Kunda with fifteen, and Saṅkoṭaka as another; / Amoghabhūti with a crore, and likewise Aghabhūtika;
another, Ekapād, with sixty, and Āraṇa with seven; / Mahābala with nine, and renowned Apasmāra;
lord of gods Nīla with ninety, and Pūrṇabhadra likewise; / and Nirṛti came along with seventy crores; / surrounded by twenty hundreds of thousands of crores,
the afflicting plagues arrived there, endowed with the power of great yoga; / bhūtas by a thousand crores, pramathas by three crores; / Vīrabhadra with sixty-four, and Vṛṣabha of great strength;

resounding with the cooing sounds of pigeons; with cloud-scraping peaks of white, enchanting, beautiful form; / radiant with many shrines of thirty [gods], displaying solitary laughter in mounds of scattered flowers;
with the path of the gods adorned on all sides by reservoirs of water furnished with canopies of thousands of blossoming blue and white lotuses; / inhabited by birds and thicket branches joined with varied patterns of flowers dropped between the pathways;
by towering aśoka trees, their branch-ends bent under the weight of clusters of dark flowers,
delightful, illuminated by bees producing delight to the ears through the song of impassioned swarms; / at night attaining oneness, through flowering tilaka trees, with the radiance of the moon;
where the tips of darbha sprouts were cropped by herds of deer standing up, awakened from sleeping in the shade.
whose clear and extensive water had its lotuses set in motion by the wind from the wings of swans,
where peacocks danced in enclosures of blooming plantains grown upon the shores of the waters; / where the earth was tinged here and there by fallen moon-spots from peacock plumes;
where in every spot flocks of joyful, gleaming, intoxicated green pigeons perched hidden.

