A place frequented in some parts by deer,
in some parts covered with variegated heaps of flowers; / in some parts with groves of trees having very sweet songs by joyful, intoxicated Kinnara women;
with trees surrounded in some places by hermitages of sages having scattered flowers strewn above; / in some places furnished with lofty, spacious jackfruit trees laden with fruit from the very roots; where siddhas lay hidden in arbors of blossoming atimuktaka creepers;
charming with the sound of golden anklets of the siddha women; / delightful, with bees clinging to the blossom clusters of the priyaṅgu tree;

with flowers of mango and kadamba variegated with swarms of bees; / with treetops shaken by winds carrying masses of flowers;
with bamboo clumps crushed in front by the elephants of the gods; / with frightened herds of female deer darting through the bushes;
bestowing liberation on embodied beings who possess discernment.
With lovely tilaka trees white as networks of moonbeams,
with aśoka trees resembling vermilion, saffron, and safflower, / and karṇikāra trees resembling gold,
piled high with masses of flowers, with very broad branches;
with the surface of the ground covered with flowers resembling collyrium powder in some places, like coral in others, / and like gold elsewhere.
Resounding with the cries of hundreds of birds on punnāga trees; bent under the weight of clusters of red aśokas; / with charming borders, a fatigue-dispelling breeze, and the play of bees on blossoming water-lilies.
